1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Chapter one, what maladaptive daydreaming actually is, how it

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,140
is measured, and who has it.

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:08,800
Here is the plain definition.

4
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:13,520
Maladaptive daydreaming, or MD for short, is compulsive, vivid,

5
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:14,920
story -like fantasy.

6
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:18,320
It is often launched on purpose, with music and

7
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:18,680
movement.

8
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,240
The person craves it, struggles to stop it, and

9
00:00:22,240 --> 00:00:26,080
lets it crowd out real life, work, relationships, and

10
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:26,400
sleep.

11
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:28,560
The idea has a short history.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,440
In 2002, Illy Summer names it after treating six

13
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:33,000
patients.

14
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:36,580
In 2011, a survey of 90 people calls it

15
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:37,620
compulsive fantasy.

16
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:41,900
In 2016 comes the first scale, tested in 45

17
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:42,460
countries.

18
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:46,580
In 2017, researchers propose formal criteria.

19
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:50,980
And in 2025, 27 experts asked for it to

20
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:53,420
be listed as a dissociative disorder.

21
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:57,760
Summer, a psychologist in Israel, defined it as extensive

22
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:02,820
fantasy activity that replaces human interaction or interferes with

23
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:05,360
study, relationships, or work.

24
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:08,680
He believed it grew from a normal childhood talent

25
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:12,920
for imagination, which hard circumstances turned into a problem.

26
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:17,740
In 2011, Bigelsohn and Shupak surveyed 90 self -identified

27
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:21,340
fantasizers and proposed the name compulsive fantasy.

28
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:25,340
79 % moved their bodies while fantasizing.

29
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,320
The distress came from losing control of the urge,

30
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:31,600
from fantasy getting in the way of real relationships

31
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,100
and goals, and from intense shame.

32
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:38,560
They estimated that fantasy filled about 56 % of

33
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:39,500
their waking hours.

34
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:43,980
Today's proposed bar from a 2025 position paper has

35
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:44,900
five parts.

36
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:49,060
First, vivid absorbing fantasy that you feel compelled to

37
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:49,900
return to.

38
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:54,260
Second, at least two extra features, preferring fantasy to

39
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:58,440
reality, pacing, or other movement, relief from bad feelings,

40
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:02,000
annoyance when interrupted, or failed attempts to stop.

41
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:04,960
Third, it lasts at least 30 minutes an episode

42
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,440
and an hour a day on most days for

43
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,060
six months.

44
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:11,520
Fourth, it causes real distress or impairment.

45
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:17,140
And fifth, nothing else like OCD, ADHD, or PTSD

46
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:18,740
explains it better.

47
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:22,880
The 2017 criteria listed features that may sound familiar.

48
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,060
It is triggered or kept going by music or

49
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:27,260
by repetitive movement.

50
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:29,760
It shows up when you are distressed or bored

51
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:31,280
and more when you are alone.

52
00:02:31,720 --> 00:02:33,240
You get annoyed when interrupted.

53
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:35,980
You prefer it to chores, study, or work.

54
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,140
And attempts to cut back keep failing.

55
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:41,500
The same paper called it a behavioral addiction to

56
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:43,920
vivid imagery that can last for hours.

57
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,260
What does it feel like from the inside?

58
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,440
In 21 in -depth interviews, people described a rewarding

59
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,940
experience built on a created fantasy of a parallel

60
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:56,740
reality with a profound sense of presence.

61
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:00,640
Their natural gift for vivid daydreaming had become a

62
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:01,820
time -consuming habit.

63
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:05,820
Being alone was necessary, and music or movement seemed

64
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:06,460
essential.

65
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:11,560
The plots follow emotional needs, especially competence, recognition, and

66
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,000
support.

67
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:16,140
Common ones are an idealized version of yourself, fame

68
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:21,060
and success, romance, wish fulfillment, alternate families, and whole

69
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:24,620
fantasy worlds that run for years, with characters aging

70
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:25,440
over time.

71
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,860
Success plots appear explicitly.

72
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:32,000
One person described scenes where, in their words, I

73
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
am someone famous who answers a question in an

74
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,300
interview in a very intelligent way.

75
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,140
Keep that one in mind.

76
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,560
We will come back to success fantasies because they

77
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:44,100
carry a specific cost.

78
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:48,300
Across these accounts, three things recur, reward, ritual, and

79
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:48,640
regret.

80
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:51,080
The reward is strong enough that a quarter of

81
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:52,640
one sample called it an addiction.

82
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:54,640
One said, I can't turn it off.

83
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:56,900
Like a drinker who promised himself this is the

84
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:57,600
last bottle.

85
00:03:58,100 --> 00:03:59,260
The ritual is deliberate.

86
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:02,760
People set up music, solitude and movement on purpose,

87
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:04,500
even while feeling compelled.

88
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:07,520
And the regret shows up as shame and secrecy.

89
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:10,520
Only 23 % had ever sought therapy.

90
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,940
Pause the video if you need to.

91
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:16,660
What three things recur in people's accounts of MD?

92
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:22,800
Reward, ritual and regret.

93
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:26,820
A strong reward, a deliberate ritual and the regret

94
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:28,100
of shame and secrecy.

95
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:31,220
For many people, simply learning that this has a

96
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:34,940
name has been, in one researcher's words, utterly life

97
00:04:34,940 --> 00:04:35,360
-changing.

98
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,160
And it is not an Internet -era invention.

99
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:42,900
An analysis of twenty classic novels, from Frankenstein to

100
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:46,320
The Great Gatsby, found the same patterns long before

101
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:47,560
the Internet existed.

102
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:51,000
The standard screen is a sixteen -item questionnaire called

103
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,140
the MDS16.

104
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:52,280
16.

105
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:55,640
It measures four things, yearning the pull to go

106
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:59,580
back, impairment, what it costs you, kinesthesia, meaning movement,

107
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:00,380
and music.

108
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:03,720
The music items were added because people with MD

109
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:06,380
kept telling researchers how central it was.

110
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:10,520
Across four countries, the most consistent core was interference

111
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:13,080
with daily life and long -term goals.

112
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:16,220
In the original study, a score of 50 or

113
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:20,080
more separated confirmed cases from controls almost perfectly.

114
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,320
Real world screening is messier.

115
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:25,780
In population studies, only 60 % of people who

116
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:28,840
screened positive were confirmed by a clinical interview.

117
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:32,800
And among adults with ADHD, the scale struggles to

118
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,400
tell the two conditions apart.

119
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:38,800
An Arabic version has also been validated, with 180

120
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:41,140
people from several Arab countries.

121
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:45,580
Officially, MD is not in either psychiatric manual, the

122
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:47,580
DSM, or the ICD.

123
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:54,440
In March 2025, 27 dissociation researchers and clinicians formally

124
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:57,420
asked for it to be listed as a dissociative

125
00:05:57,420 --> 00:05:58,020
disorder.

126
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:00,820
They argue it passes four of the five classic

127
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:03,060
tests of a valid diagnosis.

128
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:07,100
Only lab markers and family studies are missing, there

129
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:08,640
is still no official response.

130
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:17,080
In and many clinicians will not know the term.

131
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:18,580
How common is it?

132
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:22,120
The only study that confirmed cases by clinical interview

133
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:26,340
in Israel estimates about 2 .5 % of adults,

134
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,300
that is 5 in every 200 people.

135
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:33,520
It is about 4 .4 % among students, highest

136
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:37,340
between 18 and 30, with no clear female majority.

137
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:42,120
Research samples, though, are mostly women, about 76 %

138
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:43,460
across 40 studies.

139
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:47,560
That reflects who joins online communities and volunteers for

140
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,780
surveys more than who has MD.

141
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:53,520
In the general population, there is no clear female

142
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:56,940
majority, so a young man with MD is not

143
00:06:56,940 --> 00:06:57,940
an unusual case.

144
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:02,080
Surveys of students in Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and

145
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:05,460
among Sudanese medical students report much higher figures.

146
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:08,600
70, 54, and 34 percent.

147
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:10,360
None of them used interviews.

148
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:14,240
Since even stricter screens overcount by about 40 percent,

149
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:17,400
these numbers point to heavy daydreaming under study pressure.

150
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:21,380
The confirmed rate is closer to 2, 5 percent.

151
00:07:22,020 --> 00:07:24,200
It is common in some clinical groups.

152
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:28,920
About 20 percent of adults with ADHD, an estimated

153
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:33,140
25 to 40 percent of people with dissociative identity

154
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:38,700
disorder and 49 out of 100 inpatients with dissociative

155
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:39,320
disorders.

156
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:42,820
It usually starts in childhood and it is stable.

157
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:46,960
When 814 people in Italy were followed for 13

158
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,420
months, their scores barely moved.

159
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:52,640
It is a lasting pattern, not a passing phase.

160
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,400
The online community on Reddit alone has more than

161
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:58,600
121 ,000 members.

162
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:01,080
Now everyone's mind wanders.

163
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:05,540
In a study that sampled 2 ,250 adults about

164
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:08,860
a quarter of a million times, minds wandered 46

165
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:12,100
.9 % of waking hours, and the wandering seemed

166
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:14,640
to cause later unhappiness more than the reverse.

167
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:18,980
But ordinary mind wandering is spontaneous and usually not

168
00:08:18,980 --> 00:08:19,620
fanciful.

169
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:24,020
It helps with planning, creativity, and relief from boredom.

170
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:28,500
The psychologist Jerome Singer described three styles of daydreaming,

171
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:33,300
positive, constructive, collective, playful, wishful, planful, and creative, guilty,

172
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:38,540
dysphoric, obsessive, anguished fantasies and poor attentional control, being

173
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:40,700
unable to stay with either the thought or the

174
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:41,140
task.

175
00:08:41,660 --> 00:08:44,660
MD looks like the second and third combined, plus

176
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:46,000
compulsion and impairment.

177
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:50,140
And unlike fantasy proneness, it does not involve confusing

178
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:51,740
fantasy with reality.

179
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:56,400
The clearest numbers come from 156 adults diagnosed by

180
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:56,860
interview.

181
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:01,160
An immersive daydreaming factor tracked MD strongly with a

182
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:05,460
correlation of 0 .71, but ordinary mind -wandering only

183
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:10,800
weekly at 0 .31 and ADHD at 0 .23.

184
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,920
Immersion is the signature of MD.

185
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:16,780
So imagination itself is not the problem.

186
00:09:17,540 --> 00:09:20,300
Researchers treat a strong talent for immersive daydreaming as

187
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:22,020
a risk factor, not the disorder.

188
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:22,220
order.

189
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:24,620
Three markers together define the problem.

190
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:28,980
Immersion, a deliberate, story -based, emotionally vivid world.

191
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:33,900
Compulsion, yearning, irritation when interrupted, and failed attempts to

192
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:34,400
cut back.

193
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:38,180
And cost, lost work, relationships, or sleep.

194
00:09:38,740 --> 00:09:40,960
That gives you a simple test for any daydream.

195
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:43,860
Does it end in an action, like a title,

196
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,760
a script beat, or a plan?

197
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:48,700
Or does it replace the action and keep looping?

198
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:52,920
Imagination is not the enemy, unchecked immersion is.

199
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:56,320
Quick check, what are the three markers that separate

200
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:58,560
MD from ordinary mind -wandering?

201
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:07,520
Immersion, compulsion, and cost, all three together, not imagination

202
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:10,820
on its own, and roughly how many adults out

203
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:15,040
of 100 have MD confirmed by a clinical interview.

204
00:10:18,740 --> 00:10:21,780
About 2 .5 out of 100.

205
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:26,060
Self -report surveys come out far higher because screens

206
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:26,880
over count.

207
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:31,160
Researchers still argue about what MD fundamentally is.

208
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:34,280
In the next chapter, we will look through six

209
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:37,640
different lenses and see what each one explains about

210
00:10:37,640 --> 00:10:38,760
your own experience.

